# Piping into anonymous Elixir functions

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:obsidian_livebook, github: "scossar/obsidian_livebook"}
])
```

## Pipe into an anonymous Elixir function with `then/2`

[/then/2](https://elixir.hexdocs.pm/Kernel.html#then/2) pipes the first argument, `value` into the second argument, a function `fun` and returns the result of calling `fun`.

```elixir
"hello"
|> then(fn str -> str <> " world!" end)
|> IO.puts()

# Using capture syntax
"hello"
|> then(&(&1 <> " again!"))
|> IO.puts()
```

## Pipe into an anonymous Elixir function with immediate invocation

Because the pipe operator expects a function *call* on its right side, you can't pipe directly into an uninvoked anonymous function block, but you can wrap the function in parenthesis and invoke it with `.()`.

I'm guessing a bit here, but I think this is the less idiomatic approach.

```elixir
"hello"
|> (fn str -> str <> " from an invoked anonymous function" end).()
|> IO.puts

# Or:
"hello"
|> (&(&1 <> " from invoked anonymous function using capture syntax")).()
|> IO.puts
```

## Why all this piping to IO.puts?

It's fun, but mostly so that I can get output from multiple lines of code that are executed in a LiveBook cell. Maybe there's another way.

## Publish to Obsidian

```elixir
ObsidianLivebook.publish("/home/scossar/obsidian_vault", __ENV__.file)
```
